WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Last time, continuing exchange explained a steady-looking mixture.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.960
But our next mixture does not start at

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:07.760
equilibrium.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.280
Imagine a closed container containing only A.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.480
As B forms, can it start changing back

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.520
into A before the forward reaction finishes?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.400
Yes.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.040
Let us follow those two processes together and

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.960
see why their rates come to match.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:27.766
Use a simple one-to-one reaction model: one A

00:00:27.847 --> 00:00:30.806
becomes one B, and one B becomes one

00:00:31.047 --> 00:00:31.366
A.

00:00:31.927 --> 00:00:35.046
Matter stays inside, and temperature and volume remain

00:00:35.127 --> 00:00:35.606
fixed.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.446
The bars show concentration: how much substance is

00:00:39.527 --> 00:00:40.926
present per volume.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.606
In this particular model, more of a starting

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:46.566
substance means a faster reaction in its direction.

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:49.286
At the start there is A but no

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:49.766
B.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.326
So A can become B, but there is

00:00:52.407 --> 00:00:54.486
no B available to change back yet.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:57.006
As soon as B begins to form, the

00:00:57.047 --> 00:00:59.046
reverse reaction can begin too.

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.906
Each time the forward reaction gets ahead, the

00:01:02.947 --> 00:01:05.626
mixture contains less A and more B.

00:01:06.187 --> 00:01:08.586
Less A makes the forward rate fall in

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:09.226
our model.

00:01:09.707 --> 00:01:12.186
More B makes the reverse rate rise.

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:15.306
Watch the rate graph: the forward curve comes

00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:17.866
down while the reverse curve comes up.

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.186
Neither reaction needs to wait for the other

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:21.106
to finish.

00:01:21.307 --> 00:01:24.346
Eventually their average rates match, and both are

00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:25.866
still greater than zero.

00:01:26.107 --> 00:01:29.306
Then each direction replaces what the other removes,

00:01:29.387 --> 00:01:32.026
so the mixture has no overall change.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:34.746
The curves approach that balance; the graph does

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:36.826
not mark an exact finishing time.

00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:40.113
Now change the vertical axis.

00:01:40.354 --> 00:01:42.913
Concentration means how much of a substance is

00:01:42.994 --> 00:01:44.193
present per volume.

00:01:44.674 --> 00:01:47.713
On this concentration graph, A falls and B

00:01:47.794 --> 00:01:50.193
rises, then both level off.

00:01:50.674 --> 00:01:52.273
Their final heights are different.

00:01:52.754 --> 00:01:55.313
That is possible because equal rates do not

00:01:55.594 --> 00:01:57.313
require equal concentrations.

00:01:57.794 --> 00:02:02.033
Keep the quantities separate: rates meet; amounts settle.

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:05.553
These directions describe our A-only starting mixture.

00:02:05.954 --> 00:02:08.273
A different starting mixture can approach the same

00:02:08.474 --> 00:02:09.873
balance from the other side.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.860
Try a new reaction model.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.740
C and D change into each other one

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.300
for one in a closed container at constant

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:19.580
temperature and volume.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.540
Each direction gets slower when less of its

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.460
starting substance is present.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.860
The early rate from C to D is

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.740
two units.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.220
The rate from D to C is six

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.820
units on the same scale.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.700
Which substance initially increases?

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.020
Explain using both rates.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.780
Then explain how the faster direction changes as

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.380
the mixture approaches balance.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.540
Pause if you would like longer.

00:02:59.967 --> 00:03:02.846
The reverse reaction makes C at six units

00:03:03.087 --> 00:03:06.206
while the forward reaction uses C at two.

00:03:06.327 --> 00:03:10.046
So C increases overall, and D decreases.

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:13.406
With less D, the reverse reaction slows in

00:03:13.487 --> 00:03:14.046
this model.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:17.606
With more C, the forward reaction speeds up.

00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:20.446
The rates move towards balance from the opposite

00:03:20.527 --> 00:03:22.766
starting direction to our first example.

00:03:23.967 --> 00:03:26.846
A reversible reaction can run in both directions

00:03:27.087 --> 00:03:28.406
before equilibrium.

00:03:28.687 --> 00:03:31.246
In our stated model, changing amounts bring the

00:03:31.327 --> 00:03:33.326
opposing rates towards equality.

00:03:33.807 --> 00:03:36.686
Remember: rates meet; amounts settle.

00:03:37.407 --> 00:03:40.446
Next, start with a mixture already at equilibrium

00:03:40.607 --> 00:03:43.006
and ask what changes directly when we add

00:03:43.047 --> 00:03:46.126
a substance, and what the reaction does afterwards.
